title: 'The forks folder: what a note forks, and what forks it'
pid: 3a212421-b2a1-4f94-8725-76decd432e80
body: |2-
  A view of a note N shows, unasked wherever N has one, its =forksFolder= ("It forks these, or these fork it, or these fork or are forked by others here."): every mirror of N, each once, flat ('docs/forks.org', "Every mirror, flat"): every note N forks (its forkees, in N's own order), then every note that forks N and that N neither forks nor hides (its forker-only members), then every other mirror of N that N does not hide (its indirect members, joined to N only through others), the last two in the graph's and the config's order. A note that both forks N and is forked by it, a /mutual/, stands once, among the forkees. The heralds tell them apart: =bK= (N forks it), =Kb= (it forks N), =bKb= (both), neither 'b' for an indirect member. No member shows a forks folder of its own unless asked (=C-c l f= on it).

  A member claims membership exactly when N forks it, and the save sets N's =forks= to the claiming members. So:
  - typing a note into the folder makes N fork it; the view drew nothing under it, so nothing is cut from it, and the save hides none of its content, whatever you type under it (=docs/forks.org=, "Typing a note into the folder");
  - deleting a forkee stops N forking it;
  - deleting a forker-only or an indirect member hides it in N: it leaves the forks folder for the folder of what N hides outside its fork partners ("b hides these, but no fork partner shown contains them."), the last thing in the forks folder; removing it from there unhides it, and it is drawn in the forks folder again;
  - deleting a mutual stops N forking it and hides it, likewise; detaching it (=C-c s t=) only stops the fork, and it stands on as a forker, claiming nothing;
  - attaching a forker-only member (=C-c s a=) makes N fork it: a mutual; attaching an indirect member makes N fork it too;
  - deleting a member shown as "restricted" (a note the skgrepo restriction keeps out) changes nothing: the save leaves the forks and hides involving it as they are, and the member is drawn again.
  A forker-only member's record of its fork is in its own file, so the relRepo gesture refuses on it; to make it stop forking N, edit it, not N. An indirect member has no record with N, and the gesture refuses on it too.

  In a content view a forker-only member or an indirect member (a /forker as such/) is drawn as a forkee as such is: editable, unless it is a repeat or the node budget is spent, showing what it adds to N's content (its content less what N contains or hides, and less what another note of yours contains), with its own folders but a forks folder. Under it stand the hidden-in folder, of what N hides of its content ("☮ It contains these, but c hides them."), and, under a forker-only member, the hidden-by folder (=hiddenByForkerFolder=, "☮ a hides these, which c contains."), of what it hides of N's content: what that forker deleted from your note. Both are write-protected displays; the forker's hides are its owner's to edit, in its own view. Its title and body are saved when you own it and refused when it is foreign; a headline typed under it, or a node moved there, joins its content when you own it. The save never reads what a forker shows as its whole list, so nothing N contains or hides is ever removed from the forker. Moving a child out of its hidden-in folder to stand under it unhides that child in N, as under a forkee. Cutting a child from it makes N fork it (a forker as such that a cut is made from becomes a forkee too): the forker becomes a mutual, redrawn among the forkees, claiming, without its hidden-by folder (drawn under a forker only), and N hides the child unless N, or another note of yours, contains it after the save. So cutting a child from a forker and pasting it into N's own content takes that child into N, hides nothing, and makes N fork the forker, to follow what else it adds. The fork is recorded in N's file, so under a foreign N such a save is refused, as a cut from a forkee there is.

  N's hides apply to its forkers' and its indirect members' content as to its forkees': hides are symmetric. A hide of something only a forker (or an indirect member) contains stands under that member, in its hidden-in folder, and not in the hidden-outside folder, since it has a reason: the member would show it. Likewise, a child you remove from N's own content that a forker or an indirect member (one N does not hide) still contains is hidden in N, as one a forkee contains is, so that it does not come back under that member, unless another note of yours keeps it after the save, when no member shows it; and where the member is a forker, that hide is recorded no more publicly than the forker's records (its =forks= record of N and its =contains= record of the child): the forker is a partner on record for it (=glossary.org=, "hides" under "relation, relationship, member, membership, partner"). An indirect member has no record with N, so it is no partner on record, and raises no such floor.
